{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "At fixed Ne, mu, and sigma, expected heterozygosity decays with generation through a single time-homogeneous retention scale, so the H_g-versus-g curve has one effective relaxation shape set globally by the same drift term.",
      "child_relation": "Replace the constant effective population size assumption by deterministic generation-dependent effective size under sustained demographic change, so the cumulative loss rate is governed by a nonstationary product/integral over per-generation drift strengths. The target then depends on how g interacts with the demographic trend, changing the curvature of H_g across generations even when initial and current scales are comparable.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "Ne",
        "g"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Holding H0, mu, and sigma fixed, populations with the same nominal Ne but observed over longer g show faster-than-parent early loss under sustained decline or slower-than-parent early loss under sustained growth; the curvature of heterozygosity decay over g is no longer captured by one global relaxation constant."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "drift_scale",
        "expression": "1/(2*Ne)",
        "role": "baseline per-generation coalescent sampling intensity under the parent"
      },
      {
        "name": "mut_retention",
        "expression": "(1 - 2*mu)**2",
        "role": "mutation-induced retention of identity across one generation"
      },
      {
        "name": "inhom_decay_proxy",
        "expression": "g/(2*Ne)",
        "role": "parent cumulative drift exposure that would become insufficient under nonstationary demography"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when elapsed generations are long enough that cumulative drift is non-negligible, g/Ne is not tiny, and the underlying demographic trend across those generations is sustained rather than rapidly reversing; strongest at low to moderate mu where mutation does not mask drift curvature.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Training may mostly sample near-stationary cases where a constant-Ne approximation is adequate, while holdout can include longer horizons or stronger demographic trend so that heterozygosity loss departs systematically from a single homogeneous decay shape.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit of zero demographic trend, where generation-specific effective size becomes constant and the cumulative decay collapses to the current constant-Ne form.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model treats effective population size as constant over the entire observation window, collapsing all drift history into one fixed Ne even though realistic finite populations often experience sustained growth or decline that changes the cumulative heterozygosity loss rate with generation.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Effective population size is constant across generations.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Time-inhomogeneous genetic drift under deterministic demographic change modifies the cumulative probability that two sampled gene copies avoid recent coalescence, altering the generation dependence of expected heterozygosity decay even under neutrality and recurrent mutation.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "Ne",
      "g",
      "mu",
      "sigma",
      "H0"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the parent's time-homogeneous drift assumption rather than adding an unrelated process. The parent target mapping uses one constant Ne to set the full H_g-versus-g shape, so once H0, mu, and sigma are fixed, changing g only moves along a single decay family with fixed curvature. The scientific gap is that demographic history within the elapsed interval can change drift strength across generations. Under sustained decline, later generations experience stronger drift than early ones, making heterozygosity loss accelerate relative to the parent's single-scale prediction; under sustained growth, the opposite occurs. This is a changed target mapping, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the observable output H_g itself changes for the same supplied H0, nominal Ne, mu, sigma, and g when the constant-Ne assumption is relaxed to a nonstationary demographic history. The contrast is visible by varying g while holding H0, mu, and sigma fixed: the parent predicts one homogeneous relaxation shape, whereas the child predicts systematically different curvature over g once cumulative drift exposure is redistributed across generations. This matters when g spans enough generations for drift to accumulate and when mutation is not so strong that it dominates the trajectory. Limitation: with freedom_change constrained to none, the immediate refinement must be encoded as a changed assumption using existing symbols rather than introducing an explicit new demographic-trend input, so the eventual implementation will need to represent nonstationary drift in a reduced way."
  },
